===========================================================================
Make uninstall v1.9 Kent Robotti <robotti@metconnect.com> 7/2/2001

This script is used to monitor a command like 'make install',
so a record of the install can be created, which will allow
you to completely remove the package from your system later.

  make_uninstall -l                    <List packages in /var/log/uninstall/
                                        packages>
  make_uninstall -r package_name       <Remove installed package>
  make_uninstall -c package_name       <Create package_name.tgz package from
                                        /var/log/uninstall/packages/pkg_name>
  make_uninstall -i package_name.tgz   <Install tgz created package>
  make_uninstall -u                <Create record of 'make install' package>

Usually when you compile a package you do './configure ; make ; make install'.

Instead of the 'make install' step do 'make_uninstall -u'.

Example: ./configure ; make ; make_uninstall -u

You can see a listing for each installed package in
/var/log/uninstall/packages/package_name.

http://ibiblio.org/pub/Linux/utils/package "make_uninstall-1.9.tar.gz"

The above is the more general version of 'make_uninstall' for any
linux system, if you have a slackware linux system you can get a
more slackware specific version called 'pkgtool2' here.

http://www.tux.org/pub/people/kent-robotti/looplinux "pkgtool2.tgz"
ftp://ftp.tux.org/pub/people/kent-robotti/looplinux

The license for make_uninstall is GPL.
